1
00:00:00,449 --> 00:00:03,082
So we got LM Studio installed and set up.

2
00:00:03,548 --> 00:00:05,828
How can we now use an open model?

3
00:00:06,615 --> 00:00:09,232
Now, as you learned in the first course

4
00:00:09,264 --> 00:00:11,496
section, you could of course visit Hugging

5
00:00:11,608 --> 00:00:14,322
Face and there the models catalog and take a

6
00:00:14,322 --> 00:00:16,939
look at which model you personally find

7
00:00:17,276 --> 00:00:17,822
interesting.

8
00:00:18,802 --> 00:00:21,676
It's worth noting though that not all models

9
00:00:22,254 --> 00:00:25,096
are supported by LM Studio necessarily

10
00:00:25,096 --> 00:00:25,176
though.

11
00:00:25,706 --> 00:00:27,437
But you can find out by

12
00:00:28,126 --> 00:00:31,299
going to the model card of a model you might

13
00:00:31,331 --> 00:00:33,799
want to use. And then there on Hugging Face,

14
00:00:33,879 --> 00:00:37,228
you will find this Use This Model dropdown

15
00:00:37,260 --> 00:00:37,500
here.

16
00:00:38,222 --> 00:00:40,401
And if you expand this, you see the options

17
00:00:40,449 --> 00:00:41,443
you have for running it locally.

18
00:00:41,971 --> 00:00:44,055
You might need to configure the local apps

19
00:00:44,055 --> 00:00:45,433
you have installed in your system though.

20
00:00:45,753 --> 00:00:47,917
And for that, you might need to log in with

21
00:00:47,917 --> 00:00:49,167
your Hugging Face account.

22
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,212
By the way, this will not be the only way of

23
00:00:52,468 --> 00:00:54,103
finding new models. And therefore, if you

24
00:00:54,103 --> 00:00:55,529
don't have that and if you don't want that,

25
00:00:55,609 --> 00:00:56,683
that's fine. I'll show you

26
00:00:57,212 --> 00:00:58,979
a different, more straightforward way of

27
00:00:58,979 --> 00:01:00,681
finding models in a second. But that would

28
00:01:00,729 --> 00:01:02,818
be one way. So if you, for example,

29
00:01:02,834 --> 00:01:04,922
configure that you have Ollama and LM Studio

30
00:01:04,922 --> 00:01:05,725
on your system,

31
00:01:06,271 --> 00:01:08,166
you should see these options here if you can

32
00:01:08,279 --> 00:01:11,058
run this model like this with LM Studio. Now

33
00:01:11,106 --> 00:01:11,379
here

34
00:01:12,134 --> 00:01:14,656
I see nothing because indeed Gemma 3

35
00:01:15,347 --> 00:01:18,656
is supported by LM Studio, but not the raw

36
00:01:18,961 --> 00:01:19,507
version of it.

37
00:01:20,182 --> 00:01:23,153
Instead, as you learned in the quantization

38
00:01:23,153 --> 00:01:25,931
and hardware requirements section, Typically

39
00:01:25,964 --> 00:01:28,050
when running models locally, you run a

40
00:01:28,050 --> 00:01:30,361
quantized, a compressed version of that

41
00:01:30,361 --> 00:01:30,617
model.

42
00:01:31,596 --> 00:01:33,955
So on Hugging Face, we would scroll down to

43
00:01:33,987 --> 00:01:36,042
this list here to find the quantizations

44
00:01:36,234 --> 00:01:38,047
that have been created based on this model.

45
00:01:38,721 --> 00:01:40,663
And if we then take a look at some of them

46
00:01:41,193 --> 00:01:43,696
there, eventually we will find some that do

47
00:01:43,696 --> 00:01:45,141
support LM Studio.

48
00:01:46,328 --> 00:01:47,451
And you could then click on this

49
00:01:48,013 --> 00:01:48,206
to

50
00:01:48,896 --> 00:01:51,351
actually open LM Studio and start loading

51
00:01:51,399 --> 00:01:52,009
that model.

52
00:01:53,254 --> 00:01:55,152
But that's not the only way of finding

53
00:01:55,184 --> 00:01:57,532
models. The more straightforward and

54
00:01:57,581 --> 00:01:59,865
convenient way of finding models that are

55
00:01:59,929 --> 00:02:03,172
supported by LM Studio is to go back to LM

56
00:02:03,172 --> 00:02:03,708
Studio

57
00:02:04,359 --> 00:02:06,050
and then go to the settings

58
00:02:07,059 --> 00:02:08,653
and there to model search.

59
00:02:09,590 --> 00:02:11,560
Alternatively, you could use the shortcut

60
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:13,367
that's shown here, which might differ for

61
00:02:13,367 --> 00:02:15,305
you, but you can use whatever is shown there

62
00:02:15,305 --> 00:02:15,630
for you.

63
00:02:17,242 --> 00:02:19,530
And here you'll find a list of models that

64
00:02:19,595 --> 00:02:20,968
are supported by LM Studio.

65
00:02:21,559 --> 00:02:24,740
So for example, here I could use Gemma 3 and

66
00:02:24,772 --> 00:02:25,576
then I can choose

67
00:02:26,122 --> 00:02:28,693
which flavor, which version I want. Do I

68
00:02:28,693 --> 00:02:31,716
want the big one with 27 billion parameters,

69
00:02:31,812 --> 00:02:32,038
which is

70
00:02:32,634 --> 00:02:35,275
bigger to load and requires more RAM? Or do

71
00:02:35,291 --> 00:02:37,546
I want the very small one with 1 billion

72
00:02:37,546 --> 00:02:39,527
parameters, which runs on

73
00:02:40,123 --> 00:02:41,700
low performance machines as well?

74
00:02:43,487 --> 00:02:45,677
And here I'll go for the middle ground and

75
00:02:45,677 --> 00:02:47,786
choose the 12 billion parameters model, but

76
00:02:47,786 --> 00:02:49,605
it really doesn't matter. You can go with

77
00:02:49,605 --> 00:02:50,850
the very small one too, and you could also

78
00:02:50,867 --> 00:02:51,183
use a different model.

79
00:02:52,822 --> 00:02:54,602
This does not matter. It's just an example

80
00:02:54,651 --> 00:02:54,811
here.

81
00:02:55,533 --> 00:02:58,483
It's also worth noting that all these models

82
00:02:58,596 --> 00:03:01,402
you're loading through LM Studio, through

83
00:03:01,402 --> 00:03:02,332
that model search,

84
00:03:03,246 --> 00:03:06,630
will normally be quantized. For example,

85
00:03:06,678 --> 00:03:10,271
this here is a Q4 quantization, which means

86
00:03:10,639 --> 00:03:14,953
it uses 4-bit integers to reduce the amount

87
00:03:14,953 --> 00:03:17,584
of memory this model needs for running it

88
00:03:17,664 --> 00:03:20,469
locally. So no matter if you're using this

89
00:03:20,469 --> 00:03:23,370
model or some other model, when loading them

90
00:03:23,515 --> 00:03:27,025
into LM Studio, you typically use quantized

91
00:03:27,121 --> 00:03:29,686
versions simply because they are faster,

92
00:03:29,926 --> 00:03:32,122
they take up less space, and there is no

93
00:03:32,266 --> 00:03:35,280
real reason to use the unquantized one as

94
00:03:35,408 --> 00:03:38,020
explained in that quantization and hardware

95
00:03:38,020 --> 00:03:39,046
requirements section.

96
00:03:39,639 --> 00:03:41,627
So you can select whichever model you're

97
00:03:41,627 --> 00:03:43,951
interested in, and then here, right inside

98
00:03:43,951 --> 00:03:46,195
of LM Studio, you can learn a bit more about

99
00:03:46,195 --> 00:03:46,243
it.

100
00:03:46,916 --> 00:03:48,744
You learn about the number of parameters

101
00:03:49,257 --> 00:03:50,283
used by that model,

102
00:03:51,004 --> 00:03:53,328
the number of downloads, when it was

103
00:03:53,344 --> 00:03:54,418
updated, and so on.

104
00:03:55,252 --> 00:03:58,202
You find a link to the model card on Hugging

105
00:03:58,234 --> 00:03:59,356
Face, which you can click

106
00:03:59,981 --> 00:04:02,626
to be taken to that respective Hugging Face

107
00:04:02,626 --> 00:04:04,020
page of that model.

108
00:04:04,870 --> 00:04:06,826
And you also find the model README down

109
00:04:06,890 --> 00:04:07,499
there with

110
00:04:08,156 --> 00:04:09,695
more details about the model.

111
00:04:10,962 --> 00:04:13,158
You can also choose from download options,

112
00:04:13,302 --> 00:04:15,674
though for this model here, I only have one.

113
00:04:15,787 --> 00:04:17,951
And the default option in general is what

114
00:04:17,951 --> 00:04:18,319
you want.

115
00:04:19,296 --> 00:04:20,723
Though for some models,

116
00:04:21,460 --> 00:04:23,688
like for example these different versions of

117
00:04:23,721 --> 00:04:24,586
the Gemma model,

118
00:04:25,227 --> 00:04:27,616
you can actually choose from different

119
00:04:27,648 --> 00:04:29,123
quantization options here.

120
00:04:29,716 --> 00:04:32,954
And the higher this number here is, the more

121
00:04:33,114 --> 00:04:35,262
space and memory that model will take up.

122
00:04:35,583 --> 00:04:36,961
And of course, that's not just available for

123
00:04:37,026 --> 00:04:38,981
Gemma. If you take a look at some other

124
00:04:39,174 --> 00:04:42,412
models like the DeepSeek models here, though

125
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:45,425
these are slimmed-down versions of DeepSeek

126
00:04:45,762 --> 00:04:45,890
R1,

127
00:04:46,483 --> 00:04:47,317
not the large one,

128
00:04:47,846 --> 00:04:49,257
but if you take a look at models like these,

129
00:04:49,673 --> 00:04:52,111
you also find different quantization options

130
00:04:52,159 --> 00:04:52,335
here.

131
00:04:53,538 --> 00:04:56,231
These Gemma models here are a bit special

132
00:04:56,343 --> 00:04:59,517
because this QAT model, which I'm using here

133
00:04:59,549 --> 00:05:01,921
in this demo, has actually been already

134
00:05:01,953 --> 00:05:04,326
trained with quantization in mind, which is

135
00:05:04,358 --> 00:05:06,249
why there are no different options to choose

136
00:05:06,346 --> 00:05:08,253
from. But for other models, that differs,

137
00:05:08,365 --> 00:05:10,289
and you can choose exactly the version you

138
00:05:10,369 --> 00:05:10,770
want.

139
00:05:11,812 --> 00:05:13,848
You also see the size of the download here,

140
00:05:14,537 --> 00:05:16,429
so that will also be the size occupied on

141
00:05:16,461 --> 00:05:18,256
your disk once the model has been

142
00:05:18,304 --> 00:05:20,196
downloaded, because it will be stored on

143
00:05:20,228 --> 00:05:22,510
your disk so that LM Studio can it now,

144
00:05:22,870 --> 00:05:25,044
tomorrow, and so on. Of course, you can also

145
00:05:25,093 --> 00:05:26,564
delete models from your disk though.

146
00:05:27,940 --> 00:05:30,195
So here I wanna use this model. I picked it,

147
00:05:30,227 --> 00:05:32,095
I'm happy with it, and now I can click

148
00:05:32,143 --> 00:05:34,753
download here and this will download it onto

149
00:05:34,978 --> 00:05:37,446
your machine, store it in a specific folder,

150
00:05:37,948 --> 00:05:40,605
and then make it available for use here in

151
00:05:40,783 --> 00:05:43,148
LM Studio. And I'll be back once that

152
00:05:43,148 --> 00:05:44,557
download finished for me here.

153
00:05:46,050 --> 00:05:48,554
So here for me, the download finished and I

154
00:05:48,635 --> 00:05:50,537
could now click load model to

155
00:05:51,139 --> 00:05:53,163
load it into LM Studio. Because when you

156
00:05:53,163 --> 00:05:55,646
download it, it's simply stored on your hard

157
00:05:55,646 --> 00:05:57,857
drive, on your system, but you then still

158
00:05:57,889 --> 00:06:00,838
need to load it to activate it, so to say,

159
00:06:00,854 --> 00:06:03,113
to be able to use it in Element Studio. Now

160
00:06:03,177 --> 00:06:05,516
alternatively, you can also do that through

161
00:06:05,516 --> 00:06:07,263
that model picker here at the top,

162
00:06:07,888 --> 00:06:09,650
because once you have at least one model

163
00:06:09,666 --> 00:06:12,086
downloaded, if you open that picker, you

164
00:06:12,086 --> 00:06:14,169
should see that model here. And of course,

165
00:06:14,233 --> 00:06:16,028
if you have more models, you'll see more

166
00:06:16,140 --> 00:06:16,749
models here.

167
00:06:17,838 --> 00:06:20,435
And you can simply click on a model here to

168
00:06:20,965 --> 00:06:23,470
load it. By the way, if you're not in user

169
00:06:23,470 --> 00:06:25,621
mode, if you're in power user or developer

170
00:06:25,621 --> 00:06:25,991
mode,

171
00:06:26,585 --> 00:06:29,893
you will see more options here. So if I am

172
00:06:29,957 --> 00:06:33,121
in power user mode, I don't just get this

173
00:06:33,169 --> 00:06:34,245
downloads window here,

174
00:06:35,176 --> 00:06:35,513
but

175
00:06:36,124 --> 00:06:38,998
I also get more configuration options when I

176
00:06:39,175 --> 00:06:41,551
load a model. So if I click on a model here,

177
00:06:42,322 --> 00:06:43,896
which is the one that's currently already

178
00:06:43,928 --> 00:06:46,160
loaded, but I could reload it. If I click on

179
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:47,509
it here, I get more options.

180
00:06:48,168 --> 00:06:50,079
For example, I can set the context length,

181
00:06:50,272 --> 00:06:52,425
the context window size that's available.

182
00:06:53,084 --> 00:06:55,028
And we could even add more advanced options

183
00:06:55,028 --> 00:06:57,261
as well, but I'll get back to those later.

184
00:06:57,582 --> 00:06:59,478
For now, I'll go back to the user mode and

185
00:06:59,494 --> 00:07:01,679
just load the model as is with the default

186
00:07:01,679 --> 00:07:02,096
options.

187
00:07:03,205 --> 00:07:05,165
Now in the bottom right corner, you'll also

188
00:07:05,213 --> 00:07:07,832
see your system resources usage. For

189
00:07:07,848 --> 00:07:10,852
example, how much RAM or video RAM the model

190
00:07:10,852 --> 00:07:14,098
is taking up and how much work your CPU has

191
00:07:14,194 --> 00:07:14,451
to do.

192
00:07:15,172 --> 00:07:17,752
You can also click on this here to unload

193
00:07:17,880 --> 00:07:18,505
all models.

194
00:07:19,402 --> 00:07:21,469
If you click on that model picker, you'll

195
00:07:21,485 --> 00:07:23,632
also see how much memory this model is

196
00:07:23,664 --> 00:07:24,272
consuming.

197
00:07:25,106 --> 00:07:27,605
Now here I'm on an M1 Mac

198
00:07:28,294 --> 00:07:30,248
and therefore I have something that's called

199
00:07:30,313 --> 00:07:32,716
unified memory. So there is no difference

200
00:07:32,716 --> 00:07:36,321
between regular RAM and VRAM, the RAM made

201
00:07:36,321 --> 00:07:38,115
available by the graphics card. It's just

202
00:07:38,243 --> 00:07:40,999
one RAM here, which is why I have one RAM

203
00:07:41,127 --> 00:07:43,146
number here and then how much of that RAM is

204
00:07:43,146 --> 00:07:44,299
taken up by that model.

205
00:07:44,973 --> 00:07:47,058
If you're on a different system, it may

206
00:07:47,123 --> 00:07:49,385
differ whether the model was loaded into

207
00:07:49,385 --> 00:07:51,390
your regular RAM, if you don't have a

208
00:07:51,390 --> 00:07:54,358
graphics card with dedicated video RAM or a

209
00:07:54,358 --> 00:07:56,620
graphics card that's not supported, or

210
00:07:56,845 --> 00:07:59,139
whether it's been loaded into the RAM made

211
00:07:59,235 --> 00:08:01,401
available by that graphics card, the video

212
00:08:01,481 --> 00:08:01,706
RAM.

213
00:08:02,316 --> 00:08:03,984
It will simply depend on your system.

214
00:08:04,529 --> 00:08:06,984
In general, if the model is loaded into your

215
00:08:07,016 --> 00:08:08,909
regular RAM, not the video RAM,

216
00:08:09,519 --> 00:08:11,925
using it will be quite a bit slower, so

217
00:08:11,990 --> 00:08:14,477
generating the response will take a bit

218
00:08:14,493 --> 00:08:15,601
longer for the model,

219
00:08:16,180 --> 00:08:17,545
but it will still work.

220
00:08:18,509 --> 00:08:20,469
Okay, so here I got this model selected now.

221
00:08:20,855 --> 00:08:23,007
Now of course I can click on create a new

222
00:08:23,007 --> 00:08:23,425
chat

223
00:08:24,453 --> 00:08:27,249
and then start chatting with that model.

224
00:08:27,827 --> 00:08:29,900
And just to make it really clear that this

225
00:08:29,900 --> 00:08:31,731
is running locally on our system,

226
00:08:32,390 --> 00:08:32,695
I will

227
00:08:33,289 --> 00:08:34,559
disable my Wi-Fi

228
00:08:35,314 --> 00:08:37,354
so that I got no internet connection

229
00:08:38,093 --> 00:08:41,017
and still I can ask, what can you do for me?

230
00:08:41,805 --> 00:08:44,024
And send this message to this locally

231
00:08:44,121 --> 00:08:46,726
running model. And then here in LM Studio,

232
00:08:46,855 --> 00:08:48,865
you'll get back a response that's

233
00:08:48,946 --> 00:08:51,020
automatically parsed and formatted as

234
00:08:51,117 --> 00:08:54,719
Markdown, and that's therefore quite nice to

235
00:08:54,735 --> 00:08:55,041
read.

236
00:08:56,504 --> 00:08:56,730
Now I'll

237
00:08:57,550 --> 00:09:00,300
re-enable my Wi-Fi here, but we don't need

238
00:09:00,300 --> 00:09:01,876
it to use this model, of course.
